WEBVTT

00:00:00.556 --> 00:00:02.937
Empty sets create an important edge case.

00:00:02.937 --> 00:00:04.852
ANY needs at least one successful comparison.

00:00:04.852 --> 00:00:08.762
If the set is empty, no comparison can succeed, so ANY is FALSE.

00:00:08.762 --> 00:00:12.092
ALL asks whether every comparison succeeds.

00:00:12.092 --> 00:00:15.867
With no values, there is no failing comparison, so ALL is TRUE.

00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:20.807
This can feel surprising, but it follows the logic of existential and universal conditions.

00:00:20.807 --> 00:00:25.557
Connect this idea to a compliance workflow checking whether related evidence exists.

00:00:25.557 --> 00:00:28.397
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:28.397 --> 00:00:35.937
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:35.937 --> 00:00:40.332
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:40.332 --> 00:00:44.800
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
